            {
              "title": "Cha, Amenable L2-theoretic methods and knot concordance",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.1058",
              "locator": "Mixed-series homology comparison, preprint page23"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Let $W$ be a connected Hausdorff space with a supplied basis of contractible open neighborhoods, $o\\in W$, $\\pi=\\pi_1(W,o)$, and $N\\triangleleft\\pi$. Put $G=\\pi/N$ and let $k$ be any commutative unital ring. Use first-loop-first concatenation in $\\pi$. There is a canonical based normal path-class cover $p:W_N\\to W$ with $\\pi_1(W_N)=N$, whose right deck action is\n$$[\\gamma]\\cdot g=[a^{-1}*\\gamma]\\quad\\text{when }a\\in\\pi\\text{ represents }g.$$\nLet $\\mathcal R_x$ be the free $k$-module on $p^{-1}(x)$, with its induced right $k[G]$ action and transport along lifted paths. Each fiber is free of rank one as a right $k[G]$-module. Its base fiber is identified with $k[G]$ by the constant-path lift. Thus transport around a based loop $a$ is left multiplication by its quotient image $g^{-1}$.\n\nFor this specified right local system, the actual singular chain complex is naturally isomorphic to $C_*(W_N;k)$ as a complex of right $k[G]$-modules, and there is a natural isomorphism\n$$H_1(W;\\mathcal R)\\cong (N/[N,N])\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}k.$$\nThe right action on the last group is $[u]\\otimes c\\mapsto[a^{-1}ua]\\otimes c$ for $g=[a]\\in G$. A based loop $u\\in N$ with coefficient $1$ corresponds to $[u]\\otimes1$. In particular it represents zero in $H_1(W;\\mathcal R)$ exactly when it lies in the kernel of $N\\to(N/[N,N])\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}k$. Taking $N=P^s\\pi$ gives exactly the next mixed-series kernel $P^{s+1}\\pi$ with coefficient ring $k$. For a based continuous map carrying $N$ into a normal subgroup $N\\prime$ of the target fundamental group, these identifications commute with its induced homology maps and quotient-group coefficient maps. This includes every connected Hausdorff manifold, with boundary allowed, and every connected finite polyhedron. No finite CW structure, compactness, finite generation of $\\pi$, or choice principle is assumed.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "dependency_level": 0
